WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.266
Quantization lowers numerical precision so a model uses less memory and often runs more practically on local hardware.

00:00:08.266 --> 00:00:16.108
Labels such as Q2 through Q8 describe broad precision levels, but exact schemes and model behavior differ.

00:00:16.108 --> 00:00:23.102
Lower-bit quantization generally reduces file and memory size while increasing the chance of quality loss.

00:00:23.102 --> 00:00:30.520
Higher precision uses more resources and is not automatically better for every task, runtime, or machine.

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:32.851
Test the actual file.

00:00:32.851 --> 00:00:38.573
G G U F is a common local model format for llama.

00:00:38.573 --> 00:00:40.057
cpp-compatible runtimes.

00:00:40.057 --> 00:00:48.323
Compatibility depends on architecture, runtime version, tokenizer, prompt template, metadata, and acceleration backend, not only the file extension.

00:00:48.323 --> 00:00:57.012
Memory fit includes model weights, key-value cache, context, batch settings, concurrent sessions, runtime overhead, display use, and other applications.

00:00:57.012 --> 00:01:02.311
File size is therefore not a complete VRAM or unified-memory estimate.

00:01:02.311 --> 00:01:09.729
Some runtimes allow different cache precision or memory strategies, creating another quality, compatibility, and capacity tradeoff.

00:01:09.729 --> 00:01:17.995
Treat those settings as part of the tested configuration instead of assuming model quantization tells the whole story.

00:01:17.995 --> 00:01:25.413
Partial GPU offload can run some layers on the accelerator and leave others on the CPU.

00:01:25.413 --> 00:01:32.407
This may make a larger model load, but transfer and CPU work can reduce speed.

00:01:32.407 --> 00:01:36.858
A technically successful load is not an operational success.

00:01:36.858 --> 00:01:45.547
Avoid configurations that consume nearly all available memory, swap heavily, crash on long prompts, or throttle under sustained work.

00:01:45.547 --> 00:01:52.117
Compare several quantizations and sizes with realistic context, then preserve a margin for stability.
